{"type":"standard","title":"Kenroku-en","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Kenroku-en</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q998239","titles":{"canonical":"Kenroku-en","normalized":"Kenroku-en","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Kenroku-en</span></span>"},"pageid":522712,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Stone_lantern_Kenrokuen.jpg/330px-Stone_lantern_Kenrokuen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":216},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Stone_lantern_Kenrokuen.jpg/3840px-Stone_lantern_Kenrokuen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":5110,"height":3343},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1378327780","tid":"d713ff83-bf8a-11f1-a450-07ddd4608ef7","timestamp":"2026-10-04T00:30:46Z","description":"Park in Kanazawa, Ishikawa, Japan","description_source":"local","coordinates":{"lat":36.561944,"lon":136.6625},"content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenroku-en","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenroku-en?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenroku-en?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Kenroku-en"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenroku-en","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Kenroku-en","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenroku-en?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Kenroku-en"}},"extract":"Kenroku-en , located in Kanazawa, Ishikawa, Japan, is a strolling style garden constructed during the Edo period by the Maeda clan. Along with Kairaku-en and Kōraku-en, Kenroku-en is considered one of the Three Great Gardens of Japan and is noted for its beauty across all seasons, particularly in winter. Spread over nearly 25 acres, features of the landscape include meandering paths, a large pond, several tea houses, and one of Japan's oldest fountains. First opening to the public in 1871, the garden was later designated a National Site of Scenic Beauty in 1922, and subsequently received status as a National Site of Special Scenic Beauty in 1985. The grounds are open through paid admission year-round during daylight hours.","extract_html":"<p><b>Kenroku-en</b><span style=\"font-weight:normal\"> </span>, located in Kanazawa, Ishikawa, Japan, is a strolling style garden constructed during the Edo period by the Maeda clan. Along with Kairaku-en and Kōraku-en, Kenroku-en is considered one of the Three Great Gardens of Japan and is noted for its beauty across all seasons, particularly in winter. Spread over nearly 25 acres, features of the landscape include meandering paths, a large pond, several tea houses, and one of Japan's oldest fountains. First opening to the public in 1871, the garden was later designated a National Site of Scenic Beauty in 1922, and subsequently received status as a National Site of Special Scenic Beauty in 1985. The grounds are open through paid admission year-round during daylight hours.</p>"}